\chapter{Continuity Ontology \& Autopoietic Identity}
\label{chap:continuity_ontology}

\section{Overview}

The Continuity Ontology Layer governs the preservation of structural identity,
functional coherence, and autopoietic self-maintenance within the CC-MSSC v1.0
architecture. While the Structural Ontology defines the biological topology and
the Process Ontology defines metabolic and signaling dynamics, the Continuity
Ontology ensures that the system maintains its identity under perturbation,
stress, and latent-force forcing. This chapter formalizes the identity vector,
continuity invariants, degradation metrics, autopoietic repair mechanisms, and
their integration with UFSL and the master evolution equation.

\section{Autopoietic Identity Vector}

The identity vector $I(t)$ encodes the system’s structural and functional
coherence:
\[
I(t) = \left[i_1(t), i_2(t), \ldots, i_{n_I}(t)\right]^T,
\]
where each component represents:
\begin{itemize}
    \item membrane boundary integrity,
    \item cytoskeletal coherence,
    \item metabolic viability,
    \item signaling fidelity,
    \item macro-scale structural identity.
\end{itemize}

The identity vector evolves according to continuity-driven dynamics and
UFSL-modulated forcing.

