\section{Continuity Degradation Metrics}

Continuity degradation occurs through:
\begin{itemize}
    \item structural collapse,
    \item metabolic failure,
    \item signaling breakdown,
    \item resource depletion,
    \item environmental or astrophysical stress.
\end{itemize}

We define the degradation metric:
\[
\Delta I(t) = \left|1 - \overline{I(t)}\right|,
\]
where $\overline{I(t)}$ is the mean identity score.

\section{Continuity Degradation Operator}

The degradation operator $\mathcal{D}_{cont}$ is:
\[
\mathcal{D}_{cont}(I(t)) = \kappa \cdot \Delta I(t),
\]
where $\kappa$ is a continuity degradation coefficient modulated by UFSL
energy density.

\section{Autopoietic Repair Mechanisms}

Autopoietic repair is driven by:
\begin{itemize}
    \item metabolic resource allocation,
    \item structural reinforcement,
    \item signaling restoration,
    \item UFSL-modulated repair acceleration.
\end{itemize}

The repair operator is:
\[
\mathcal{R}_{cont}(I(t)) =
\lambda \cdot I(t)\left(1 - I(t)\right),
\]
where $\lambda$ is a repair coefficient.

\section{Continuity-Driven Damping}

Continuity-driven damping suppresses excessive forcing when identity is
threatened. The damping factor is:
\[
\eta(t) = 1 - \tanh\left(1.5 \Delta I(t)\right).
\]

This factor modulates UFSL tensor contraction:
\[
\Theta_{mod}(t) = \eta(t) \cdot \Theta(t).
\]

\section{Continuity Contribution to the Master Evolution Equation}

Continuity dynamics influence the master evolution equation:
\[
\frac{d\mathbf{X}}{dt} =
\mathbf{F}(\mathbf{X}, t) +
\mathbf{K}(\mathbf{X})\Theta \cdot
\exp\left(-\gamma \mathcal{C}_{identity}(t)\right),
\]
where:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $\gamma$ is a continuity damping coefficient,
    \item $\mathcal{C}_{identity}(t)$ is the continuity constraint function.
\end{itemize}

Continuity contributes to:
\begin{itemize}
    \item baseline operator modulation,
    \item UFSL tensor suppression,
    \item structural reinforcement,
    \item process stabilization.
\end{itemize}

\section{Continuity Stability Requirements}

To ensure stability:
\begin{itemize}
    \item identity degradation must remain bounded,
    \item autopoietic repair must exceed degradation,
    \item UFSL energy must remain below $\mathcal{E}_{max}$,
    \item damping must activate under continuity threat.
\end{itemize}

These requirements guarantee that the system maintains coherence under
multiscale forcing.

\section{Continuity–UFSL Interaction}

Continuity and UFSL interact bidirectionally:
\begin{itemize}
    \item continuity suppresses UFSL forcing,
    \item UFSL accelerates continuity repair,
    \item both contribute to global stability,
    \item both enforce bounded energy dynamics.
\end{itemize}

This closed-loop interaction is formalized in Chapter 10.

